<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jingdezhen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Changnan, Fuliang, Porcelain Capital</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jingdezhen is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">Porcelain</a> Capital for producing ceramics for at least 1,000 years. Founded as Changnan under the Jin dynasty, it took its present name in 1004 from the era name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhenzong-of-song">Emperor Zhenzong of Song</a>, under whom its porcelain first rose to fame. Close to deposits of pottery stone and kaolin and to pine forests for fuel, it was the largest centre of Chinese porcelain by the 14th century, making qingbai ware, blue and white from the 1330s and famille rose under the Qing. From the Ming onwards its official kilns made imperial porcelain for the court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> forces destroyed its 9,000 kilns in 1855.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some worked for the government: the Ministry of Public Works hired artisans and labourers for temporary service, and the imperial kilns at <b>Jingdezhen</b> made the famous blue and white porcelain, while the Dehua factories in Fujian made export porcelain for European tastes by the late 16th century.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
